00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:57.130 position:63%
One way to generate the Thue Morse
sequence is to take the digits in binary

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:01.930 position:63%
SOPHIE: of increasing numbers and just
add the digits and look at it modulo 2,

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:03.710 position:63%
and this will give the sequence.

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:07.830 position:63%
One way to generate the
sequence is iteratively.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:09.410 position:63%
So you'll start with A,

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:16.070 position:63%
and then in each iteration, you'll replace
every A with AB and every B with BA.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:22.450 position:63%
In a rowing boat, a lot of rowers
will row with just one oar,

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:25.390 position:63%
SOPHIE: either on their left
side or their right side,

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.320 position:63%
and the Thue Morse sequence gives us
the optimal configuration of left and

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:30.160 position:63%
SOPHIE: right-siders.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:38.320 position:63%
So if you take the Thue Morse sequence
and look at the As or Bs

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.980 position:63%
in the cube-number positions,
so we'll start at zero,

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:44.570 position:63%
SOPHIE: you are equally
likely to have an A or B.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.080 position:63%
A really cool fact about the sequence
is if you take any two distinct positive

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:56.240 position:63%
integers, alpha and beta, and you
construct a continued fraction according

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:00.960 position:63%
to the Thue Morse sequence,
then the resulting fraction is always

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.950 position:63%
transcendental. So that means it's never
a solution to a polynomial equation.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:08.880 position:63%
There are some really cool summation
results we can get from the Thue Morse

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:14.040 position:63%
sequence. So if instead of looking at
As and Bs we consider zeros and ones,

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:18.550 position:63%
and we consider the nth element of the
Thue Morse sequence with the first element

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:19.580 position:63%
SOPHIE: being t0,

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:26.120 position:63%
we'll find that we can use the sequence
to find a sum equalling pi squared,

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:30.610 position:63%
and another one equalling 8 times
the Riemann zeta function at 3.

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:34.710 position:63%
And there's a more complicated formula
for the Riemann zeta function at any

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:37.330 position:63%
odd integer.
Mentioned in Matt's first video,

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:39.620 position:63%
that I'm gonna reiterate here
'cause it is very cool.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.800 position:63%
If you were to take
the Thue Morse sequence,

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:46.450 position:63%
and whenever you get an A
you rotate half a circle, so pi radians,

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:51.690 position:63%
SOPHIE: and whenever you get a B, you walk one unit and then rotate a sixth of a circle,

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:55.860 position:63%
so pi over 3 radians,
you end up tending to the Koch snowflake.

00:06:56.490 --> 00:07:00.730 position:63%
As a final fun fact, I think we might be
mispronouncing the Thue Morse sequence for

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:01.700 position:63%
SOPHIE: the entire video.

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:06.120 position:63%
Some sources say that it's not Thue,
it's actually Thue, but who's taking note?

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:10.130 position:63%
Right. We all agree Thue Morse
sequence is incredible.

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:14.040 position:63%
MATT: But now we need to generalise
this sequence to more people.

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:18.560 position:63%
Now, very recently someone emailed in
to the podcast I do with Bec Hill,

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:24.220 position:63%
A Problem Squared, and they asked if
they've got 14 people in their fantasy

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:29.100 position:63%
MATT: sport league, what is the fairest
order for them to select from the draft

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:35.310 position:63%
in? And it turns out there is one way you
can define the Thue Morse sequence which

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:39.360 position:63%
MATT: does generalise, and it
preserves those properties,

00:07:39.670 --> 00:07:43.990 position:63%
and it's through the lens of
imagining it as a kind of duel.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:47.850 position:63%
[gentle music]

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:49.560 position:63%
Here's a new way to frame the problem.

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:53.340 position:63%
You and I are having some
kind of duel competition.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:55.510 position:63%
I'm gonna go with darts. There you are.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:57.950 position:63%
And can you see in
[laughs]

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:00.300 position:63%
MATT: the vast distance
a hypothetical dartboard?

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:00.970 position:63%
SOPHIE: Yes.

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:02.950 position:63%
So [laughs]
here's the game.

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:04.090 position:63%
MATT: Don't throw that. [laughs]

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:06.880 position:63%
The game is first person
to get a bullseye wins.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:07.630 position:63%
SOPHIE: Okay.

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:10.300 position:63%
Now, this could be playing golf,
hole in one,

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:12.270 position:63%
- Right,
- shooting basketball half-court.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:14.320 position:63%
MATT: Something with a small
probability of success.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:16.290 position:63%
But there is an advantage to going first,

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:17.690 position:63%
- Of course.
- 'cause you might get it first.

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:19.070 position:63%
SOPHIE: Right, and then
you don't even get a turn.

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.780 position:63%
MATT: Bingo. So there's like a
1% chance you get it, let's say.

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:22.770 position:63%
SOPHIE: Let's say 2%, come on.

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:24.800 position:63%
MATT: 99%. Okay, 2% chance you get it;

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:26.980 position:63%
98% chance I get it.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:30.120 position:63%
So the rule is we take
turns throwing the darts,

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:34.420 position:63%
and at each point we calculate
whoever has the smallest probability

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:38.410 position:63%
of having won already gets to go next.

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:39.250 position:63%
SOPHIE: Right.

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:42.130 position:63%
MATT: Which sounds a bit
ridiculous, but it kinda works.

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:44.450 position:63%
SOPHIE: And all this is
determined beforehand, right?

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:46.370 position:63%
It's not dependent on what's

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:47.440 position:63%
- No,
- happened during a game.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:49.310 position:63%
SOPHIE: Predetermined
based on the probabilities.

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:53.100 position:63%
MATT: Yeah. In fact, you can
pre-crunch every single outcome

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:53.470 position:63%
SOPHIE: Right

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:57.410 position:63%
MATT: by looking back and saying, what are
the probabilities A and B are both won,

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:58.560 position:63%
and picking the smaller one.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:01.720 position:63%
So at the very beginning, you've
got a 2% chance of winning.

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:02.570 position:63%
SOPHIE: Yep.

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:06.560 position:63%
MATT: But on the second go, I've
got a 0% chance of having won,

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:10.300 position:63%
and you've got a 2% chance of
having won, so I get to go next.

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:15.240 position:63%
But my probabilities are now where
there's a 98% times 2% chance I have won,

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:16.050 position:63%
SOPHIE: Right

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:18.580 position:63%
MATT: which is still a
little bit smaller than 2%.

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:19.130 position:63%
SOPHIE: Yes.

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:20.000 position:63%
MATT: So I get to go again.

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:21.230 position:63%
SOPHIE: Okay.

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:26.440 position:63%
MATT: And I then have a 98% squared
plus 98% cubed chance of having one

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:27.230 position:63%
SOPHIE: Right

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:30.730 position:63%
MATT: which has just tipped over bigger than two, so now you get to have the next go.

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:31.470 position:63%
SOPHIE: Okay.

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:35.520 position:63%
MATT: And we both build up these
long series of probabilities.

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:38.330 position:63%
SOPHIE: And then it goes me, you, you, me.

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:41.760 position:63%
Would you believe it generates
the Thue Morse sequence?

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:42.970 position:63%
- Brilliant.
- It's so great.

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:46.410 position:63%
And more importantly, can be generalised.
And it works.

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:50.990 position:63%
The duel method does generalise for any
number of players in a fair share

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:56.790 position:63%
Thue Morse sequence. This was first
pitched in 2013 by Joshua Cooper and Aaron

00:09:56.830 --> 00:10:02.780 position:63%
MATT: Dutle in a paper called
Greedy Galois Games, and it's great.

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:05.600 position:63%
It works. I actually first came across it,

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:10.340 position:63%
it was an answer on Math Stack Exchange
by user

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:16.420 position:63%
MATT: kram1032, and not only did they
explain the duel concept, but they also

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:21.500 position:63%
provided some Python code.
So I just downloaded that and used it,

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:23.180 position:63%
MATT: and it works great.

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:26.150 position:63%
So now, as well as the
classic n equals 2 case,

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:28.890 position:63%
I could say about n equals
3 for three people choosing.

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:32.730 position:63%
We've got four people choosing,
five, and I could just keep going,

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:35.280 position:63%
and here's the one that was requested, 14.

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:38.250 position:63%
Although it kinda looks like alphabet
soup at this point, and you might think,

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:41.390 position:63%
"Ah, surely there's a neat way to
visualise this in a way that makes it

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:42.270 position:63%
easier to use," [laughs]

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:46.300 position:63%
and don't worry.
That is a problem that solved itself.

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:50.170 position:63%
MATT: So I used Kram's code to work
out the sequence for 14 players.

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:53.560 position:63%
We recorded the podcast episode with
that solution, had a lot of fun,

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:59.370 position:63%
released that, accidentally opened
the door for listeners coming up with

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:04.930 position:63%
audio and visual demonstrations
of how it works in practice.

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:08.190 position:63%
Kinda regret that.
I don't wanna relive all the remixes.

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:09.460 position:63%
MATT: I'll link to the episode below.

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:11.170 position:63%
You can check it out on your own time.

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:15.200 position:63%
But there was one visualisation that
I thought was particularly neat.

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:17.340 position:63%
So this is the visualiser
that Damien made.

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:20.020 position:63%
We'll put it on, do you fancy
the blues scale?

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:20.610 position:63%
SOPHIE: Oh, yeah.

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:25.450 position:63%
and we'll speed it up a little, and
this is what it does.

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:27.250 position:63%
[upbeat music]
It's kinda fun.

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:28.620 position:63%
- Funky.
- It's kinda funky.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:33.770 position:63%
MATT: And you can see it going around
the circle, which I think is quite neat,

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:39.890 position:63%
but it kind of makes it a little hard
to follow, I found, some of the logic.

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:42.060 position:63%
MATT: So what I'm gonna do is stop that.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:43.680 position:63%
I took Damien's code,

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:47.100 position:63%
- Okay
- me and a robot friend of mine

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:50.160 position:63%
MATT: altered it a
little to make it linear,

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:52.260 position:63%
and now it looks like this.

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:53.270 position:63%
SOPHIE: Okay.

00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:54.190 position:63%
MATT: So,

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:56.420 position:63%
how many notes would you like?

00:11:56.830 --> 00:11:58.510 position:63%
SOPHIE: Should we do eight,
just make it a scale?

00:11:58.550 --> 00:11:59.470 position:63%
MATT: Eight in the major scale?

00:11:59.510 --> 00:11:59.810 position:63%
SOPHIE: Right.

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:00.500 position:63%
MATT: Yeah, right. Cool.

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:01.890 position:63%
And I, I've got it linear.

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:02.410 position:63%
I'll set it going.

00:12:02.450 --> 00:12:03.370 position:63%
[upbeat music playing]

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:04.360 position:63%
And now it goes...

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:06.510 position:63%
I think it's easier to
kinda follow the sequence.

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:07.330 position:63%
SOPHIE: Right.

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:11.140 position:63%
MATT: And I've got it to change
colour each time it finishes a round,

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:14.940 position:63%
because once it gets more chaotic, it's hard to keep track of when you've finished.

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:18.470 position:63%
'Cause there's always this
whole permutation on n.

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:21.480 position:63%
It's hard to keep track of when
it switches to the next one.

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:22.950 position:63%
So there you go.

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:26.580 position:63%
But what's particularly interesting
about this, now it's not chaotic,

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:29.700 position:63%
but it's a little hard to
see the logic behind this.

00:12:30.130 --> 00:12:31.420 position:63%
At the very beginning,

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:34.020 position:63%
it's surprisingly straightforward.

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:38.530 position:63%
When I set this going at the beginning,
you'll see first of all it counts all the

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:39.400 position:63%
way up.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:40.890 position:63%
Then it comes all the way back down again.

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:43.890 position:63%
Then does it in the other
direction, and then back up again.

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:45.330 position:63%
Okay. After that it gets more complicated.

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:48.280 position:63%
So apparently just that
beginning is called snake draft.

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:49.810 position:63%
- What's...
- Or serpentine draft.

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:50.650 position:63%
MATT: Okay, what's that?

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:54.170 position:63%
So my board game nerd friends
are like, "Oh yeah, snake draft."

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:57.510 position:63%
That's where you go all the
way through all the players,

00:12:57.550 --> 00:12:59.450 position:63%
and then you come all the way back up again, and you just go backwards and forwards.

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:00.540 position:63%
Oh, snaking. Yeah, yeah.

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:02.080 position:63%
Well, it's easy to do.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:03.420 position:63%
Yeah. And the issue

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:03.830 position:63%
[laughs]

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:06.960 position:63%
with some of the generalised
Thue Morse sequences for higher

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:09.940 position:63%
Right unless you've got it
written out on a big bit of paper,

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:11.550 position:63%
it's real hard to follow.

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:14.260 position:63%
Yeah. Snake draft, easy, up and
down, up and down, up and down.

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:18.080 position:63%
So I thought we might do,
like, an easy version of

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:21.290 position:63%
Thue-Morse. A middle
ground between the two.

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:21.630 position:63%
In the style.

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:22.490 position:63%
In the middle ground, yeah, yeah.

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:24.980 position:63%
Between snake and full Thue.

00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:28.840 position:63%
So if you watch, you get snake in
one direction all the way down.

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:29.900 position:63%
Yeah All the way back.

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:31.810 position:63%
That's easy. And then you
do it from the other end,

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:33.310 position:63%
all the way up and all
the way back down again.

00:13:33.350 --> 00:13:35.360 position:63%
Sure. Okay. Then middle out.

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:36.880 position:63%
Okay. Outside back in.

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:38.840 position:63%
Then it starts getting chaotic.

00:13:38.930 --> 00:13:43.490 position:63%
But what you can actually do is snake
all the way down and back.

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:45.710 position:63%
MATT: Then the other
direction, up and down.

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:48.130 position:63%
Right. Then do middle out, back in.

00:13:48.170 --> 00:13:53.610 position:63%
And then do out, in, out.

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:57.750 position:63%
Yeah. And if not, it's fair enough that
that order is not gonna be why you're

00:13:57.790 --> 00:14:00.330 position:63%
MATT: Oh, yeah. You got
other issues to blame. Right,

00:14:00.370 --> 00:14:02.080 position:63%
Than the order you
picked in at that point.

00:14:02.390 --> 00:14:06.490 position:63%
So I wanna call that hydra draft.
Because multiple snakes.

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:07.420 position:63%
MATT: We got four snakes.

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:08.530 position:63%
- Nice.
- Yes. So there you are.

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:10.960 position:63%
If you just want a practical
solution, hydra draft.

00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:13.920 position:63%
First of all, everyone picks an order
and then you reverse that.

00:14:13.990 --> 00:14:15.850 position:63%
Then you do the same thing from
the back to the front,

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:17.540 position:63%
MATT: reverse it back down to the back.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:20.960 position:63%
Then you go from the middle out,
reverse that from out back in again.

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:24.840 position:63%
Then the same thing but this time it's
out to in, and then in to out.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:28.170 position:63%
MATT: And that's our attempt to make this
as fair as possible while being easy to

00:14:28.210 --> 00:14:30.520 position:63%
remember, but just how fair is it?

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:35.120 position:63%
This is a plot of hydra draft showing the
percentage advantage of whichever player

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:38.610 position:63%
has the greatest advantage compared to
the player with the absolute worst.

00:14:38.650 --> 00:14:40.450 position:63%
MATT: And you can see in the first pass,

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:44.310 position:63%
that's all the way up through everyone,
then the first player's just got a great

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:47.950 position:63%
advantage. Then the second half is when
you go the reverse order all the way back.

00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:49.750 position:63%
It brings it back to not quite perfect.

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:52.590 position:63%
And then outside in, inside
out, inside out, outside in.

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:56.620 position:63%
So that's all four waves back and
forth, and you can see it's not bad.

00:14:56.870 --> 00:15:00.270 position:63%
But what if we compare it to good
old snake draft, just back and forth,

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:00.850 position:63%
back and forth?

00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:03.660 position:63%
Well, the first two are indistinguishable
because they're identical,

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:04.780 position:63%
MATT: and that makes sense.

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:07.250 position:63%
And then you can see there is some drift.

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:08.090 position:63%
Oh, and this is what we're looking at.

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:11.970 position:63%
In terms of percentages, snake draft
is just about 4%,

00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:14.130 position:63%
MATT: whereas hydra
draft is just under 1%.

00:15:14.170 --> 00:15:19.130 position:63%
It's not bad, but what about the reigning
unbeatable champ of Thue-Morse?

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:20.660 position:63%
MATT: Well, it's the same as hydra draft.

00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:23.200 position:63%
I mean, in fact it's
identical for most of it.

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:25.910 position:63%
At the end here it
drifts ever so slightly.

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:27.350 position:63%
They're barely different.

00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:30.620 position:63%
And I have to admit that technically if
these were items of equal value,

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:32.310 position:63%
MATT: all three would
be indistinguishable.

00:15:32.350 --> 00:15:35.230 position:63%
In fact, if they were linear decrease,
you wouldn't see a difference.

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:40.100 position:63%
I've had to go for a quadratic drop-off in
quality to get this tiny difference here.

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:42.840 position:63%
And if we do it for more
rounds, so let's reset.

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:43.330 position:63%
There we go.

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:47.460 position:63%
Twice as many rounds just between
Thue-Morse and hydra draft,

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:50.880 position:63%
again it only just starts
to drift at the very end.

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:53.600 position:63%
And for you pedants, because
it's a quadratic drop-off,

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:57.530 position:63%
it's even closer back here just because
the quadratic thing rescales all the

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:00.250 position:63%
values. Eh, the point is
it's very, very close.

00:16:00.290 --> 00:16:01.090 position:63%
In fact, here's,

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:01.490 position:63%
[laughs]

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:05.720 position:63%
here's the first 24 rounds
or 12 back-and-forths,

00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:07.590 position:63%
and you can see only at the end there.

00:16:07.630 --> 00:16:10.060 position:63%
Now, this is a fraction of a % difference.

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:14.920 position:63%
Hydra draft stays very close
to the ideal Thue-Morse.

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:18.630 position:63%
So there you are, hydra draft
for all your fair sharing needs.

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:21.560 position:63%
And if you really wanna
follow the details exactly,

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:29.030 position:63%
I have taken Kram's code, and Claude and
I have exported a PDF for

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:32.620 position:63%
MATT: any number of people that
you want a fair share between,

00:16:32.990 --> 00:16:35.430 position:63%
as long as that number is 24 or smaller.

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:37.400 position:63%
You can do it yourself if you want.

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:40.630 position:63%
And that's really where
this video should end.

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:42.510 position:63%
We have the practical solution.

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:43.680 position:63%
Although that said,

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:48.360 position:63%
I would like to point out one quick
quirk behind how the code actually works,

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:50.700 position:63%
'cause it's not using
the duel probabilities.

00:16:50.890 --> 00:16:54.340 position:63%
Very interesting. If you read through
Kram's answer on Stack Exchange,

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:59.550 position:63%
you will see lots of mentions of 1 minus
epsilon, and then to various powers,

00:16:59.590 --> 00:17:04.620 position:63%
and we were very quick to switch
from 2% accurate to 98% inaccurate,

00:17:04.770 --> 00:17:08.070 position:63%
but that working out is raising
two things in brackets to a power,

00:17:08.110 --> 00:17:11.670 position:63%
and of course you're gonna
get binomial coefficients.

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:15.110 position:63%
And down here it gets
very Pascal's triangly,

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:17.930 position:63%
and that's what Kram
actually uses in their code,

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:22.670 position:63%
because the duel method we were looking at
only gives you the sequence in the limit,

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:23.990 position:63%
whereas this gives it straight away.

00:17:24.030 --> 00:17:27.790 position:63%
So that's how you write your code, and
you can see here binomial coefficient.

00:17:27.830 --> 00:17:32.500 position:63%
And you take, for all players, the
indexes of all their turns so far,

00:17:32.830 --> 00:17:34.620 position:63%
and then you gradually ratchet up.

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:38.790 position:63%
Column just means you increase
values until you break all ties,

00:17:38.830 --> 00:17:40.470 position:63%
and I'm not doing the explanation justice.

00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:41.330 position:63%
You can check it out.

00:17:41.370 --> 00:17:44.130 position:63%
But it's a quick deterministic
way to get the actual sequence,

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:49.340 position:63%
and because additional choose functions
go up to higher order polynomials,

00:17:49.490 --> 00:17:53.360 position:63%
you're basically ratcheting up how
fast the value of the things drops off,

00:17:53.470 --> 00:17:57.390 position:63%
and the higher order ones become
less and less likely tiebreakers,

00:17:57.430 --> 00:17:58.720 position:63%
so it prioritises linear.

00:17:58.850 --> 00:18:03.200 position:63%
It's very clever, and I'll link to it if
you wanna grab Kram's code and calculate

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:04.640 position:63%
some sequences for yourself.

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:08.160 position:63%
When you do actually work them out,
you quickly realise, at least I did,

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:11.500 position:63%
N equals 2 goes on forever, never repeats.

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:13.100 position:63%
N equals 3

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:17.120 position:63%
starts for a bit and then
falls into a repeating pattern.

00:18:17.470 --> 00:18:20.570 position:63%
N equals 4 starts for a bit,
falls into a repeating pattern.

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:22.720 position:63%
In fact, for any number,

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:27.200 position:63%
and I've checked up to 24, which I'm
pretty sure is the biggest number,

00:18:27.770 --> 00:18:32.700 position:63%
this always happens, and I do
not understand that pattern.

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:33.810 position:63%
Are you ready to be angry?

00:18:33.850 --> 00:18:34.550 position:63%
[laughs]

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:37.330 position:63%
Always. Spoiler, we both
ended up pretty angry.

00:18:37.370 --> 00:18:39.570 position:63%
If you wanna see it, it's
on the second channel,

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:44.410 position:63%
because I showed Sophie in great detail
how while the case for N equals 2 never

00:18:44.450 --> 00:18:47.360 position:63%
repeats, the other ones all
do fall into looping patterns,

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:50.030 position:63%
but we could not work out why or how

00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:50.370 position:63%
[laughs]

00:18:50.110 --> 00:18:52.280 position:63%
or what the structure is
or how to predict them.

00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:54.640 position:63%
It's infuriating and intriguing.

00:18:54.750 --> 00:18:55.770 position:63%
Link in the description.

00:18:55.810 --> 00:18:56.490 position:63%
Check it out.

00:18:56.530 --> 00:18:57.670 position:63%
If you work out an answer,

00:18:57.710 --> 00:18:58.350 position:63%
